      "Language": "spa",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To analyze the perception of coronary risk and health care practices in a group of Mexican women, from a gendered perspective."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MATERIALS AND METHODS",
          "content": "Mixed methods: survey of 140 women; nine in-depth interviews to women with coronary disease; eight semi-structured interviews to physicians."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "ANALYSIS",
          "content": "proportions contrast for quantitative data; and procedures of grounded theory for qualitative information."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "More than 50% of women don't know their coronary risk and how to reduce it. Despite having information about heart disease, vulnerable women with chest pain sought medical attendance less than non-vulnerable women (p=0.0l); and are blamed by physicians. Women consider they lack sufficient information about how to reduce the risk of coronary disease, and blame themselves when ill."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "There are vulnerability conditions in women that modulate a low perception of their being at risk, and the scarcity of health care practices."
        }
      ]},
